          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you (in the manner of a sister).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane explicitly presents her love for St. John as a true emotional state, while qualifying it as sisterly rather than romantic. The avowal is the expression's clear dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"The avowal itself does not undertake marriage, travel, missionary work, or any future obligation. Jane's nearby offers to assist St. John are separate and expressly distinguished from this statement of sisterly love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your curate, if you like, but never your wife.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"This is a deliberate explanatory avowal within an argument, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged utterance. It supplies Jane's reason for trusting St. John despite refusing his terms.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No substantial core force remains outside T/P/E. The sisterly qualification identifies the kind of affection being avowed, but does not create a distinct additional mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is arguing with St. John after refusing to become his wife. She explains that she would have risked much by accompanying him because she admires and trusts him and loves him as a sister. The expression is therefore a reasoned avowal of non-romantic, familial or friendly affection, contrasted with the marital love he does not offer and she does not feel for him.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The target itself undertakes nothing. In the surrounding exchange Jane separately offers to accompany St. John as an assistant or figurative sister, while refusing marriage. St. John rejects that arrangement as unsuitable and proposes that she assist a married missionary instead. Jane then denies having made a binding promise and leaves open whether she will go to India at all.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the supplied text as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. This classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passages strongly establish the avowal's sisterly rather than romantic framing and show the immediate dispute and rejection of Jane's proposed arrangement. They do not independently settle every broader question about the durability or exact psychological composition of her affection.\"}"
        }
      ],
